---
title: "Auto-Generate Columns"
---

Column definitions can be generated automatically from `rowData`, removing the need to define them upfront. This is useful when working with dynamic or unknown data shapes.

When `autoGenerateColumnDefs` is set to `true`, the keys from the first row of `rowData` are read and a column is created for each one. No `columnDefs` are needed:

{% gridExampleRunner title="Auto-Generate Columns" name="nested-objects" exampleHeight=300 /%}

```js
const gridOptions = {
    autoGenerateColumnDefs: true,
    rowData: myData,
};
```

## Column Generation

The first row of `rowData` is inspected and columns are created based on the value types found:

- **Primitive values** (strings, numbers, booleans, bigints, Date instances) — a leaf column is produced.
- **Plain objects** — a column group is produced, with nested keys recursed into. The `headerName` for each column is derived from its key name.
- **Arrays** and **null/undefined** values — a leaf column is produced by default.
- **Functions** — skipped.

For example, this row data:

```js
rowData: [
    { user: { name: 'Alice', age: 30 }, score: 100 },
]
```

The following column definitions are produced:

```js
columnDefs: [
    {
        headerName: 'User',
        children: [
            { field: 'user.name', headerName: 'Name' },
            { field: 'user.age', headerName: 'Age' },
        ],
    },
    { field: 'score' },
]
```

## Configuration

An `AutoGenerateColumnDefsConfig` object can be passed instead of `true` to control how different value types are handled:

```js
const gridOptions = {
    autoGenerateColumnDefs: {
        objectValues: 'recurse',
        arrayValues: 'include',
        nullValues: 'include',
    },
    rowData: myData,
};
```

{% interfaceDocumentation interfaceName="AutoGenerateColumnDefsConfig" /%}

## Updating Row Data

Columns are regenerated each time `rowData` is set. Only the keys from the first row are used, so if the new data has a different shape, columns are updated accordingly.

By default, column order matches the key order of the first row. Existing column positions can be preserved when the data shape changes by setting `maintainColumnOrder` to `true`:

```js
const gridOptions = {
    autoGenerateColumnDefs: true,
    maintainColumnOrder: true,
};
```

See [Maintain Column Order](./column-updating-definitions/#maintain-column-order) for details.

When `rowData` is set to `[]` or `null`, rows are cleared but column definitions are preserved. To reset columns, they should be cleared explicitly:

```js
api.setGridOption('columnDefs', []);
```

{% note %}
Updating data via transactions (`applyTransaction` / `applyTransactionAsync`) does not trigger column generation.
{% /note %}

## Customising Generated Columns

Properties set via [Default Column Definitions](./column-definitions/#default-column-definitions) are applied to auto-generated columns in the same way as manually defined ones. This is the simplest way to configure common properties like filtering or resizing across all generated columns.

For per-column customisation, the `processAutoGeneratedColumnDefs` callback can be used to modify, reorder, or replace the generated columns before they are applied. The callback receives `params.colDefs` (the generated definitions, which may include `ColGroupDef` entries for nested objects) and `params.rowData`. The returned array is applied as the final `columnDefs`.

